WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
Tokens should be transmitted only over protected channels in production.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:14.120
A token-bearing request should be treated as sensitive while it moves between client, service, proxy, gateway, and hosting infrastructure.

3
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:19.660
Transport choices should match the value of the token and the sensitivity of the resource it can access.

4
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:27.860
Authorization headers, secure cookies, and backend-mediated patterns have different exposure and CSRF implications.

5
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.000
Authorization headers are common for API clients.

6
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:39.420
Cookies can integrate with browser sessions but require careful cookie settings and request-intent protection for unsafe browser workflows.

7
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.760
Backend-for-frontend designs can reduce direct browser handling of sensitive tokens in some architectures.

8
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:51.800
Browser storage such as localStorage and sessionStorage is not a trusted secret boundary.

9
00:00:51.900 --> 00:01:02.940
It may be useful for some application state, but teams should be cautious about storing sensitive tokens where browser-side code, extensions, or client-side dependencies may interact with them.

10
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.480
Browser applications require a deliberate token-handling model.

11
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:12.760
Mobile, desktop, service, and browser clients have different storage and retrieval risks.

12
00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:18.140
A server-side service may use managed identity or protected configuration.

13
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.440
A mobile client may rely on platform storage.

14
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.300
A browser client has a very different exposure profile.

15
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:29.080
One token storage rule rarely fits every client type.

16
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:39.680
Tokens should not be placed in URLs, query strings, analytics data, screenshots, referrer paths, frontend configuration, public repositories, or downloadable assets.

17
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:46.340
These locations are commonly copied, logged, cached, or shared as part of normal operations.

18
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:55.980
Cookie-based token handling requires review of Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite, domain and path scope, lifetime, and CSRF behavior.

19
00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:05.360
A cookie may be convenient, but the application must understand when the browser sends it and how server-side logic validates user intent for state changes.
